WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.280
Movie sequels get a bad rap, but every now and&nbsp;
then, one comes along that totally blows its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.760
predecessor away. Which comic-based&nbsp;
franchise found its direction with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.160
the help of a morally ambiguous love&nbsp;
interest? Keep watching to find out!

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.200
"Gremlins" is a classic, but it's also a dark,&nbsp;
cynical little film. It establishes an idyllic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.720
"It's a Wonderful Life"-esque setting and then&nbsp;
has ugly little monsters destroy the whole thing.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.440
"Gremlins 2: The New Batch" is a wholly&nbsp;
different animal. It moves the setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.040
to a high tech skyscraper in New York City,&nbsp;
introducing a far zanier cast of Gremlins who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.480
wreak a much sillier sort of havoc, thanks to&nbsp;
a geneticist whose experiments lead to a much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.000
wider variety of gremlin shapes and sizes.&nbsp;
"Gremlins" was fun, but as Key and Peele&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.640
pointed out, "Gremlins 2" is completely&nbsp;
bananas in a way that makes it awesome.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.840
“This is G2, people. We writing&nbsp;
Gremlins 2. It should be a party!”

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.160
And even all these years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.720
it’s exactly what Hollywood should do&nbsp;
whenever it's time to "drop a deuce."

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.160
Chris Hemsworth has always been charismatic&nbsp;
and funny as Thor Odinson, the Asgardian God&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:18.480
of Thunder and the Avengers' resident himbo.&nbsp;
In fact, he's probably the best part of 2011's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:23.360
"Thor," a movie that has a lot going for it,&nbsp;
but never quite lives up to its potential,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.400
despite the work of Hemsworth and a&nbsp;
star-making turn from Tom Hiddleston as Loki.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.680
A mix of mythic fantasy, superhero action,&nbsp;
and fish-out-of-water romantic comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.320
it remains less than the sum of its parts,&nbsp;
and is perhaps a bit clunkier than it ought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:44.480
to be. The 2013 sequel "Thor: The Dark World"&nbsp;
seemed to double-down on this clunkiness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:51.280
with arguably the MCU's worst villain ever and a&nbsp;
MacGuffin that made no sense, even for the MCU.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:58.000
Then came the third movie, "Thor: Ragnarok," and&nbsp;
finally things got on track. Shifting directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.400
from Kenneth Branagh to Alan Taylor&nbsp;
to Taika Waititi, these movies clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:08.160
reflected whoever was in the chair. "Ragnarok"&nbsp;
brought a Waititi-esque, unorthodox buoyancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.480
to the series that felt like an injection&nbsp;
of buttery popcorn directly into the vein.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.920
Thor and Loki are relocated far away from both the&nbsp;
overwrought fantasy of Asgard and the mundanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.680
of Earth, setting the second act of the movie&nbsp;
instead on the weird junkyard planet of Sakaar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:32.000
ruled by Jeff Goldblum's campy Grandmaster. The&nbsp;
MCU had never seen anything like this before. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.840
the first time, Thor and Loki had a plot as fun&nbsp;
as their characters, plus the Hulk, played by Mark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:43.760
Ruffalo and Valkyrie, played by Tessa Thompson&nbsp;
memorably came along for the ride. The stakes were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.600
as high as ever — or higher, considering Asgard&nbsp;
was being threatened with complete destruction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.880
Nevertheless, the storytelling in "Ragnarok"&nbsp;
allowed for a lot more fun and a level of knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.320
ridiculousness that helped the whole "mythic space&nbsp;
gods" concept go down just a little bit easier.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.240
"I just couldn't help myself.”
 
“I know.”

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:03.040
“I'm a trickster."

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.560
"Yes. So mischievous."

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.960
David Ayer's 2016 "Suicide&nbsp;
Squad" was a mess. To start with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.920
it was the third film in the DCEU, arriving&nbsp;
the year before "Justice League." That meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:21.600
Ayer had the unenviable task of introducing&nbsp;
an entire team of incarcerated supervillains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.560
in a world where audiences had barely met any&nbsp;
of the superheroes, let alone their enemies.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.840
Even though "Suicide Squad" spends at least half&nbsp;
its run time on multiple sequences designed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.560
introduce the team, most of the characters&nbsp;
remain sketchy and underdeveloped at best.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.560
When the plot finally does get going,&nbsp;
it doesn't make a whole lot of sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.600
Furthermore, main villain Enchantress, played by&nbsp;
Cara Delevingne, was one of the villains Amanda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:50.480
Waller, played by Viola Davis, had recruited&nbsp;
for the team. So it really calls into question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.200
why her idea of a supervillain task force&nbsp;
ever seemed like a good idea to anybody.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.480
“Needless to to say, the&nbsp;
whole thing was a bad idea.”

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:05.120
Despite being just plain terrible, "Suicide Squad"&nbsp;
did uncover a tremendous hunger for an offbeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.120
film about these DC antiheroes. In the aftermath&nbsp;
of "Batman v Superman," Warner Bros. took footage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.720
from Ayer's somber film and put together a fun,&nbsp;
breezy trailer that went viral. It was as if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:21.840
someone had made a fan-produced cut of "Suicide&nbsp;
Squad" as a comedy. But the resulting tug-of-war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.680
between director and studio ultimately resulted&nbsp;
in a disjointed, unsure-of-itself debacle.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.840
Enter the king of "offbeat." James Gunn's 2021&nbsp;
"The Suicide Squad" with the all-important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.240
definite article in the title is a whole&nbsp;
lot better. It brings in a bunch of new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:44.720
team members like Bloodsport, played by Idris Elba&nbsp;
and Peacemaker, played by John Cena, all funnier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:50.160
and more well-developed as characters than anybody&nbsp;
from the first film. It also capitalized on Margot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.880
Robbie as Harley Quinn, who had been the best part&nbsp;
of the first movie, and then had another movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.920
in between — "Birds of Prey" — to develop as a&nbsp;
character and gain new fans. With a better script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:04.800
better cast, better direction, and a clearer&nbsp;
sense of the DC Extended Universe as a whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:10.640
it's no surprise that "The Suicide Squad" became a&nbsp;
vast improvement over its "The"-less predecessor.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:17.440
A live action/CGI family movie based on the&nbsp;
classic children's character Paddington Bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.760
is exactly the sort of film you'd expect&nbsp;
to get made these days. What nobody saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.040
coming was that 2014's "Paddington" turned&nbsp;
out to be absolutely delightful, retaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:32.640
the quaint charm and warmth of the original&nbsp;
stories. It also boasted a truly stellar cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.520
with Ben Whishaw as the voice of the bear,&nbsp;
Sally Hawkins and Hugh Bonneville as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.880
couple that welcomed him into their family,&nbsp;
and Nicole Kidman as a villainous taxidermist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:49.920
The movie charmed both children and adults,&nbsp;
earning a truly impressive 97% on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.960
"Paddington" was so beloved by its audience&nbsp;
that it is all the more impressive that 2017's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:02.240
"Paddington 2" turned out to be even better than&nbsp;
the first film. It earned an almost-unheard-of 99%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.880
on Rotten Tomatoes that ranks it among&nbsp;
the most acclaimed films of all time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:12.720
Consider this: "Paddington" and "Paddington&nbsp;
2" have a higher cumulative Rotten Tomatoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:18.160
score than "The Godfather," at 97%,&nbsp;
and "The Godfather II," at 96%.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:23.760
The sequel, centered on Paddington being&nbsp;
framed for a crime and sent to prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.320
might sound like a bit of a bummer. However,&nbsp;
as the little bear's charisma wins over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.960
hardened inmates, it becomes the sort of&nbsp;
film that renews your faith in humanity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:38.720
humor, love, and kindness. With Hugh Grant and&nbsp;
Brendan Gleeson joining the already impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:44.000
cast, "Paddington 2" might be as perfect a&nbsp;
family film as you're ever likely to see.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.880
"Magic Mike" is a Steven Soderbergh drama about&nbsp;
a male stripper who doesn't want to be a stripper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:57.120
anymore. "Magic Mike XXL," directed by Gregory&nbsp;
Jacobs with Soderbergh as a producer, is a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.080
about the joys of being a male stripper. While&nbsp;
there was a lot of fun to be had in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:08.000
movie, the sequel puts that fun at the forefront.&nbsp;
It portrays a kind of easygoing, comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.280
masculinity that isn't threatened by putting&nbsp;
one's body on display for the pleasure of others.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:14.560
“That’s the Mikey I remember!”

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:20.480
Unsurprisingly, the biggest fans of "XXL" were&nbsp;
women, who flocked to the movie in groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:25.520
almost as if they were going to an actual&nbsp;
male strip club, albeit one populated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:31.120
some of the most handsome actors in Hollywood.&nbsp;
Visual pleasure is central to film as a medium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.800
and few films have made that pleasure as&nbsp;
central, or as visceral, as "Magic Mike XXL."

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:43.120
While 2014's "Godzilla" was a huge improvement&nbsp;
over the previous attempt to recreate the Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:48.960
monster for American audiences, it didn't exactly&nbsp;
set the world on fire. Godzilla looked great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.240
but the monsters he fought were&nbsp;
generic and bore little resemblance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:57.040
to the unforgettable foes he faced in&nbsp;
the original Japanese series of films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:01.920
Furthermore, the soldier played by Aaron&nbsp;
Taylor-Johnson was equally milquetoast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:06.640
It was about as forgettable of a human&nbsp;
protagonist monster movie as we've ever seen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:11.440
This was a problem that was made even more glaring&nbsp;
by the lack of screentime for the giant lizard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:16.960
everybody had paid to see. Nevertheless, the movie&nbsp;
was enough of a hit for Legendary Pictures to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:21.520
forward with their plans to make Godzilla&nbsp;
the center of a "Monsterverse" franchise.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.440
The next film in that franchise was 2017's "Kong:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:30.000
Skull Island," but it was a prequel that&nbsp;
didn't even involve Godzilla directly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.800
A direct sequel to the 2014 film didn't arrive&nbsp;
until "Godzilla: King of the Monsters" in 2019.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.520
Fortunately, it arrived with everything&nbsp;
Godzilla's previous effort was missing.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.960
The classic monsters Mothra, Rodan, and King&nbsp;
Ghidorah all made their return to kick off a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:52.400
globe-spanning giant monster battle royale, with&nbsp;
the kind of monster-fighting action Godzilla fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:58.880
crave. "King of the Monsters" also brought in Kyle&nbsp;
Chandler, Vera Farmiga, and Millie Bobby Brown to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:05.040
lead the human cast as a family being torn apart&nbsp;
in the midst of the apocalyptic monster conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:10.240
Whether this Americanized series is your cup of&nbsp;
tea or not, it's hard to deny that "Godzilla:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:14.320
King of the Monsters" improves on&nbsp;
"Godzilla" on basically every level.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.160
"House of 1000 Corpses," the feature&nbsp;
directing debut of rocker Rob Zombie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:25.680
was a fun movie for horror fans, so over the top&nbsp;
that it played more like an ultra-violent cartoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:32.160
than a serious film of any sort. The titular&nbsp;
house, occupied by the murderous Firefly family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.880
became a treacherous last stand for two young&nbsp;
couples with the misfortune of ending up there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:41.760
on Halloween night. Zombie's tribute to&nbsp;
the sort of low-budget grindhouse horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.080
movies he grew up on, the film never&nbsp;
really attempted to be more than that.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:48.480
Nevertheless, the film was a success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:53.600
so Zombie jumped at the opportunity to make a&nbsp;
sequel, becoming considerably more ambitious.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:59.280
"The Devil's Rejects" opens with the destruction&nbsp;
of the Firefly house in a police raid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:04.880
and the movie focuses on three family members&nbsp;
who escape and go on the lam. While every bit as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:11.040
violent as its predecessor, "The Devil's Rejects"&nbsp;
abandons the cartoonish tone for an atmospheric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:18.160
1970s-inflected road movie with a soundtrack of&nbsp;
classic Southern rock. If horrific violence isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:23.600
your thing, this movie probably still isn't for&nbsp;
you — but if it was the overall ridiculousness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:29.120
of "House of 1000 Corpses" that turned you off,&nbsp;
"The Devil's Rejects" might be exactly your thing.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.240
Which Wolverine movie was "Logan" an&nbsp;
improvement on? How about practically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:38.720
all of them? The X-Men movie franchise&nbsp;
first introduced Hugh Jackman as Logan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:44.400
the nigh-indestructible mutant with metal claws&nbsp;
in 2000’s “X-Men”. Also known as Wolverine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:51.200
he had his own solo films before James Mangold&nbsp;
came along: 2009's "X-Men Origins: Wolverine"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:56.800
and 2013's "The Wolverine." However, this&nbsp;
Western-tinged goodbye to the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.600
was absolutely transcendent to the point&nbsp;
where some regard "Logan" as not just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.640
best film in the X-Men franchise, but&nbsp;
the best superhero movie of them all.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:10.960
In any case, "X-Men Origins:&nbsp;
Wolverine" is a nonsensical mess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:17.040
and "The Wolverine" is merely a pretty good&nbsp;
superhero action movie. Differently, "Logan" is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:22.880
genuinely moving story about an aging hero dealing&nbsp;
with his own mortality and the apparent loss of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:29.440
the hope he and his fellow X-Men once fought for.&nbsp;
Into this apocalyptic landscape arrives Laura,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:34.560
a young girl in need of protection, who has&nbsp;
far more in common with Logan than he expected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.960
Naturally, she brings his long-buried&nbsp;
heroic spirit back to the fore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:44.800
and the old man once known as Wolverine makes&nbsp;
one last stand. Owing as much to Westerns as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:50.000
it does to the X-Men movies, "Logan" is&nbsp;
about as good as superhero films can get.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:55.120
It took the "Fast and Furious" movies a&nbsp;
few installments to figure out exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.120 --> 00:12:00.640
what it wanted to be. The first film, 2001's&nbsp;
"The Fast and the Furious," was a relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:06.320
straightforward crime drama set in the world of&nbsp;
street racing, starring Paul Walker, Vin Diesel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:13.440
Michelle Rodriguez, and Jordana Brewster. Only&nbsp;
Walker returned for 2003's "2 Fast 2 Furious,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:17.440
which filled out the cast with Tyrese&nbsp;
Gibson and Chris "Ludacris" Bridges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:22.480
for another story about crime and racing.&nbsp;
The third movie, "The Fast and the Furious:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:27.680
Tokyo Drift" left all of the previous&nbsp;
characters behind, in service of a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:32.800
about an American teen, played by Lucas&nbsp;
Black, involved in street racing in Japan.

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:38.800
"Tokyo Drift" ended with a cameo from Vin&nbsp;
Diesel, however, and "Drift" director Justin Lin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:42.800
would turn out to be instrumental&nbsp;
in shaping the franchise's future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:46.720
He would return — as would Diesel&nbsp;
and Walker — for the fourth film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:51.040
"Fast &amp; Furious," and 2011's "Fast&nbsp;
Five" brought it all together.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:56.160
Justin Lin directed once again,&nbsp;
and with Walker, Diesel, Brewster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:01.920
Tyrese and Ludacris, the franchise was finally&nbsp;
firing on all cylinders. Also along for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:07.200
ride was Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson as a US&nbsp;
Government Agent hunting the protagonists.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:10.880
"The men we're after are professional runners.&nbsp;
They like speed and are guaranteed to go down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:13.520
the hardest possible way, so make&nbsp;
sure you got your funderwear on."

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:19.680
"Fast Five's" over-the-top heist plot put the&nbsp;
hot rods to new use and became a huge hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:23.600
but it also crafted a blueprint for&nbsp;
future installments of this series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:29.280
each going bigger than the last. Even still,&nbsp;
"Fast Five" is probably still the best — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:33.840
there's not many franchises that can say&nbsp;
the fourth sequel was their best film.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:40.160
The 1968 George Romero classic "Night of&nbsp;
the Living Dead" remains a great film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:45.040
both on its own merits and as the birth of&nbsp;
the modern zombie genre that stands today.

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:47.840
"They're coming to get you, Barbara."

00:13:48.800 --> 00:13:52.480
It’s a cross-media cash cow that&nbsp;
extends from "The Walking Dead"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:58.640
to "Left 4 Dead" to "Army of the Dead." Despite&nbsp;
an effectively grim atmosphere and talented cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.840
however, its budget was tiny and ambitions small.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:07.760
Although the zombie problem appears in "Night"&nbsp;
to be widespread, the movie is focused entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:13.040
on a small group of people holed up in a rural&nbsp;
farmhouse. They are attempting to hold the dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:17.840
at bay with only vague hints that something far&nbsp;
greater is going on in the rest of the world.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:23.840
By the time Romero returned to the zombie&nbsp;
narrative for 1978's "Dawn of the Dead,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:28.240
he had more resources to play with.&nbsp;
After a brutal opening showing how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:33.040
the zombie infestation has devastated&nbsp;
low-income communities in Philadelphia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:37.680
the movie follows two cops and a pair of&nbsp;
TV journalists as they flee the city and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:44.160
find refuge in a suburban mall. Although the main&nbsp;
cast is even smaller than that of his 1968 film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:50.320
Romero's reality now feels much bigger, and the&nbsp;
threat that much more real. Although the group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:55.840
manages to secure the mall against the hordes,&nbsp;
this apocalyptic temple to capitalism wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.840 --> 00:15:01.520
made to serve as a fortress. The tension becomes&nbsp;
palpable as the living dead storm the gates.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:06.800
1995's "Bad Boys" was the&nbsp;
debut` movie from Michael Bay.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:12.880
Yes, the rock star music video filmmaker who&nbsp;
would re-shape Hollywood with his glossy visuals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:17.680
adrenaline-pumping spectacle-over-story&nbsp;
aesthetic, and belief that when all else fails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:21.520
just blow something up. It all began,&nbsp;
for better or worse, with this one.

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:27.440
"Bad Boys" stars Will Smith and Martin Lawrence&nbsp;
as two reckless cops protecting a witness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:31.600
and trying to find a large amount of heroin&nbsp;
that disappeared from their police station.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:38.240
The movie combines comedy and over-the-top action&nbsp;
into a frothy thrill ride in cinematic form.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:40.880
It's a style Bay would perfect in the years to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:45.040
come with crowd-pleasing spectacles&nbsp;
like "The Rock" and "Armageddon."

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:50.960
By the time he made "Bad Boys II" in 2003,&nbsp;
Bay was riding high at the top of his game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:56.080
and that's reflected in the product. "Bad Boys&nbsp;
II" was bigger in scale than the first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.080 --> 00:16:01.200
from the explosions to the amount of missing&nbsp;
drugs. On the other hand, it didn't forget the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:06.560
importance of Lawrence and Smith's chemistry and&nbsp;
the appeal of their characters. Its introduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:12.720
of Gabrielle Union as Syd added a whole new level&nbsp;
of conflict. While "Bad Boys" was a fun time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:15.600
every facet of the film was&nbsp;
heightened in "Bad Boys II,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:20.880
striking a balance that kept it from being too&nbsp;
big or loud, making it simply fun to watch.

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:22.480
“Yeah, I could see that.”

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:30.080
Although it was a monstrously huge hit at the&nbsp;
time, 1989's "Batman" is, in retrospect, a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:36.560
of an odd duck. It's a superhero film, sure, but&nbsp;
outside of the Christopher Reeve Superman series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:42.640
superhero films weren't really much of a defined&nbsp;
thing yet. "Batman" contains a surprising amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:48.320
of '80s action movie DNA, which pulls it away&nbsp;
from the conventions of comic book storytelling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:53.360
Meaning, there’s a villain revealed to be&nbsp;
responsible for the hero's parents' deaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:58.000
the hero revealing his secrets to his love&nbsp;
interest, a climactic fight in a tower that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.560
ends with the villain falling to his death.&nbsp;
On the other hand, director Tim Burton put a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:09.120
lot of his oddball gothic style into the film. It&nbsp;
became distinctive enough to briefly foreshadow a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:14.720
superhero film revolution that wouldn't truly&nbsp;
take hold until more than a decade later.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:18.320
By the time he directed 1992's "Batman Returns,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:23.840
Burton had a lot more freedom to bend the&nbsp;
project to his own aesthetic and narrative whims.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:29.680
Consequently, "Batman Returns" is also an&nbsp;
odd duck, but one with a much clearer vision.

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:35.440
It's set at Christmas, with lots of atmospheric&nbsp;
snow and statues of angels. The new villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:41.520
the Penguin, played by Danny DeVito, is a former&nbsp;
circus freak whose henchmen are all weird carnies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:47.680
The grotesquery of the Penguin is juxtaposed&nbsp;
with the far more appealing, morally ambiguous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:53.280
Catwoman, played by Michelle Pfeiffer, who's&nbsp;
both an enemy and a love interest for Batman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:58.960
Christopher Walken also appears as evil&nbsp;
businessman Max Schreck, giving one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:05.440
most Walken-esque performances of his life. The&nbsp;
whole thing is a weird, spooky, campy delight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.800
and you can tell it's exactly&nbsp;
what Burton wanted it to be.

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:14.720
Visionary director James Whale didn't want&nbsp;
to make a second "Frankenstein" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:19.360
even though the first had been a smash hit. The&nbsp;
studio finally convinced him by letting him do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:23.680
pretty much whatever he wanted. The&nbsp;
result was "Bride of Frankenstein,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:29.120
an arch, deliberately campy outing that's&nbsp;
generally regarded as the first horror comedy.

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:34.720
Whale paired the high-strung Dr. Frankenstein&nbsp;
with a new scientist named Dr. Praetorius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:39.760
a proud degenerate who gleefully defies the&nbsp;
laws of nature. He also gave the Monster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:44.000
the power of speech, which he had in the&nbsp;
novel but lacked in the original film.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:47.200
“Good. Good.”

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:52.080
There's still plenty of pathos in "Bride,"&nbsp;
particularly the extremely dark ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:57.760
but it's balanced with a levity that the original&nbsp;
lacks, making for a much stronger film. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.760 --> 00:19:02.800
Whale's last "Frankenstein" movie, and is widely&nbsp;
regarded as the highlight of the franchise.

